":["E4"],"decision":"advisory","drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan quote: 'CI's full run passes because tests/scripts/test_alert_dedup.py inserts scripts/ on sys.path at import time, and that side effect leaks into every later-collected module in the same session.'","tests/scripts/conftest.py:32-38 performs the `_REPO_SCRIPTS_DIR ... sys.path.insert(0, _REPO_SCRIPTS_DIR)` — this is the actual source of the leak.","tests/scripts/test_alert_dedup.py:29-30 docstring: 'tests/scripts/conftest.py puts that directory on sys.path, which is also what makes the modules' bare sibling imports (canary_bridge -> alert_dedup) resolve.' — the test file itself does not insert the path.","Substantive diagnosis (session-wide sys.path leak masks the subset failure) is correct; only the source-file attribution is wrong, and no AC depends on the filename."],"finding":"The 'Why it matters even though CI is green' section attributes the sys.path side-effect to `tests/scripts/test_alert_dedup.py` (\"tests/scripts/test_alert_dedup.py inserts scripts/ on sys.path at import time\"), but the insert of repo-root scripts/ actually lives in tests/scripts/conftest.py:32-38 — not in the test module. test_alert_dedup.py's own docstring (line 29-30) states 'tests/scripts/conftest.py puts that directory on sys.path.' The named file does not perform the insert the plan credits it with.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fc355226e24f87bec","impact":0.85,"is_primary":null,"location":"Report section 'Why it matters even though CI is green'","norm_id":"n842309cfa45d4fd7","priority":0.7438,"reason":"default-advisory","scenarios":["A reader tries to confirm the masking mechanism by opening test_alert_dedup.py and finds no sys.path insert, undermining confidence in the diagnosis."],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"Replace 'tests/scripts/test_alert_dedup.py inserts scripts/ on sys.path at import time' with 'tests/scripts/conftest.py inserts scripts/ on sys.path at collection time (lines 32-38)'.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.875,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"contradicts_reality","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Replace AC3's verify with a check that actually distinguishes sibling imports — e.g. reuse the new AST guard from AC4 to enumerate bare imports resolving to a scripts/ or tests/ module, and assert the set equals the five classified rows.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T12","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T6","T9"],"criteria":["E6"],"decision":"advisory","drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Claim: 'lists no bare sibling import outside the five rows above'","The grep denylist covers only a handful of stdlib modules; sibling modules are identified by living in scripts/ or tests/, which this command does not test"],"finding":"AC3's proving command does not actually verify its own claim. `grep -rn \"^import \\|^from \" scripts/*.py | grep -vE \"rebar|__future__|collections|pathlib|typing|dataclasses\"` lists ALL top-level imports minus a small stdlib denylist — it will emit many non-sibling imports (os, sys, json, subprocess, third-party libs), so it cannot 'list no